+
+
+## Dobe maintained headless build (development)
+
+The opt-in `dobe` feature adds a private CLI identity, explicit filesystem roots,
+embedded pinned text fonts and refusal of editor delegation, plugins and external
+video encoders. Native documents and default upstream builds retain their format.
+This source integration is not full workspace, runtime or release qualification.
+
+Dobe also exposes the read-only `file.inspect_sound` command. It runs the native
+WAV, Ogg Vorbis and MPEG audio decoders under byte/sample limits, refuses damaged
+or discarded tails and nonfinite samples, and reports exact decoded metadata.
+It does not import into the document or open an audio device. The default importer
+retains its existing tolerant behavior. Complete runtime admission still needs
+embedded image checks, sound metadata comparison and expanded mixing budgets.
+
+The Dobe audio exporter now uses checked native timeline expansion and strict
+source decoding. `file.inspect_soundtrack` reports the same checked layout and
+range-specific mix work without writing audio. Excess placements, envelope
+clones, instance-continuity scans, Start/Stop matching, truncated native recursion,
+output frames or weighted sample work return errors before creating an export.
+Default playback keeps its original tolerant behavior. This is an audio foundation;
+composed geometry, shaping, runtime registration and installed qualification remain open.
+
+Dobe `text.inspect_layout` captures the selected embedded face and the actual
+harfrust glyph runs and skrifa drawing pass used by native authoring. It reports
+family/style substitution, missing characters, notdef glyphs, drawing failures,
+clusters, positions, line metrics and ink bounds without changing the document.
+The Dobe authoring path bounds text to 8,192 characters, 32,768 shaped glyphs and
+250,000 outline commands. Stored native outlines remain unchanged on load.
+The native layout still shapes one run per paragraph; complete mixed-direction
+itemization is not implemented. Actual runtime text admission and imported-outline
+comparison remain separate work.
+
+`text.inspect_stored` additionally selects a stored text block by scene or symbol,
+layer, covering keyframe and element ID. It compares the actual regenerated native
+path and box metrics with the stored outline without repairing or changing it.
+This makes stale or substituted cached glyph geometry observable before revision.
+
+The native geometry layer now has opt-in shared work scopes for path scans,
+flattened points, morph work, nine-slice output and path walking. Nested scopes
+cannot reset a budget; workers must explicitly enter the same shared scope.
+Checked primitives latch failure and stop allocating; callers must consume only
+`Budget::run`'s successful result, before publishing any file. Actual transformed
+controls are checked, and native flatten/morph/slice/walk caps become errors.
+Default calls outside a scope preserve their existing geometry and fallback rules.
+
+The private Dobe `file.inspect_frame` command now enters checked native evaluation.
+It bounds expanded timelines, layer/element scans, instance continuity, recursive
+bounds, motion/armature helpers, clips/groups, metadata copies and bitmap decoding.
+Composed transforms and output paths enter the same geometry scope. Refused work
+returns an error without publishing output or repairing the document. Repeated
+bitmap instances share one fresh decode per evaluation; header dimensions and
+aggregate decoded pixels are bounded before output allocation.
+
+The CPU renderer now has a checked single-frame route with shared stroke, buffer,
+raster and helper work budgets. Rayon workers enter the captured geometry and
+render scopes explicitly, replacing any unrelated waiting task's scope and
+restoring it afterward. Stroke sampling/dash fallbacks and truncated filter extents
+are errors. Masks, filtered groups, coverage and compositing consume the same
+budget. Dobe PNG creation and the MCP picture tool use checked computation before
+encoding or publication. Default upstream rendering remains unchanged.
+Each frame establishes its context before evaluation, including an outer batch
+task executed while another frame waits for its nested Rayon workers.
+
+The read-only evaluation report still distinguishes evaluation from rendering and
+complete media integrity: it does not itself rasterize. GIF, sprite-sheet, video
+and vector exports still require checked integration, and multi-frame operations
+need shared operation limits and publication qualification. Complete typed runtime
+and installed Animate qualification remain open. No full renderer/export or
+runtime release claim follows from the single-frame route.
+
+
+Dobe PNG sequences now use a bounded aggregate render/geometry budget while each
+frame retains its individual limits. They render one frame at a time, retain a
+bounded encoded PNG set, and finish all computation and path preflight before
+writing destination files. A late compute failure no longer leaves the first
+64-frame batch published. The existing default upstream sequence implementation
+is unchanged. A final filesystem write failure can still leave partial output;
+this route is not a multi-file atomic transaction. Complete runtime publication
+and the other export formats remain unfinished.
+
+
+Dobe GIF and sprite-sheet exports now use the checked sequence route with shared
+operation limits. GIF quantizes and encodes one frame at a time into a bounded
+writer, including a checked trailer. Sprite sheets keep one bounded RGBA8 canvas
+and the current frame; padding and transparent backgrounds preserve native pixel
+semantics. The JSON map identifies Dobe Animate, and `.png` output names prevent
+image/map aliasing. All computation and path preflight finish before publication.
+Final filesystem writes are not atomic; runtime staging and recovery remain open.
+SVG, Lottie and HTML serialization still require checked integration.
+
+
+Dobe SVG export now uses checked evaluation and bounded native serialization.
+Geometry, variable-stroke expansion, mask scans, filter ramps, gradient work and
+embedded-image encoding share checked counters; text buffers reject before growing
+past 64 MiB. Invalid composed numbers, failed bitmap encoding and truncated filter
+extents return errors before publication. Known SVG format differences are returned
+as notes. Serialization is verified separately from browser raster fidelity, which
+remains explicitly unverified. Lottie/HTML and complete runtime admission remain open.
+
+Checked SVG text and encoded-output buffers grow capacity geometrically within
+their byte cap. Small formatting fragments reuse capacity instead of forcing a
+reallocation and copy on every append. Output byte limits remain unchanged.
+
+
+Dobe Lottie export uses checked native evaluation and variable-stroke outlining
+before building the native frame tracks and hold keyframes. Every frame retains
+its evaluation and geometry limits. The export accepts 1–600 movie frames, reserves
+at most 512 MiB of cumulative buffer work, and limits helper work to 20 million
+units. The reservation includes evaluated display copies, bezier conversion, mask
+copies, track keys, names, draw-order edges and JSON materialization before their
+allocation. JSON reservations deliberately overcount unchanged frames; these are
+work limits, not measurements of peak memory. Serialized output is limited to
+64 MiB using a fallible bounded writer. Transformed vertices, tangents, gradient
+coordinates and stroke widths cannot silently become zero or disappear when they
+exceed finite coordinate bounds.
+
+The native animation structure and known feature-loss report are retained. Output
+metadata identifies Dobe Animate. The command reports
+`lottie_serialization_budget_verified=true` and `player_fidelity_verified=false`.
+Successful serialization does not establish Lottie-player pixel equivalence or
+exhaustive feature-loss reporting. All frames finish before any destination write;
+final filesystem I/O still requires runtime staging for atomic publication.
+HTML serialization, complete stored-document admission and runtime integration
+remain open. Animate is not yet registered or packaged in the product runtime.
+
+
+Dobe HTML export now runs the native scene/symbol baker inside an isolated checked
+evaluation operation. This scopes the actual guided-layer, motion-guide, camera,
+armature and variable-stroke paths; it does not substitute flattened movie frames
+for interactive timelines. Up to 6,000 frames across reachable timelines may be
+baked. Stored metadata, action lists, source scripts, intern tables, paths, paint,
+frame content and JSON copies reserve work before allocation. Retained buffer
+work is limited to 512 MiB and helper work to 256 million units. These counters
+are conservative reservations, not measurements of peak memory or CPU time.
+JSON and HTML text each have a 64 MiB cap; JSON less-than escaping happens while
+writing into that cap. Small text fragments reuse capacity.
+
+Source scripts compile from a private copy with refreshed caches, so an earlier
+cached compilation cannot override edited source text. Embedded PNGs check their
+headers and decode in the current operation; embedded WAV/Ogg/MP3 sounds use the
+complete native decoder and must match stored format/rate/channel/frame metadata.
+The checked operation cannot inherit another document's bitmap cache. Sounds
+omitted by the sound=false option are not decoded. These checks do not establish
+complete stored-document admission or complete PNG integrity.
+
+The native player, interactive timeline structure and known feature-loss notes
+remain intact. Generator metadata identifies Dobe Animate. The response sets
+`html_serialization_budget_verified=true` and `browser_fidelity_verified=false`.
+Browser canvas pixels, runtime interactions beyond the tested player fixtures,
+exhaustive feature-loss reporting and dynamic instance expansion remain separate
+qualification requirements. Construction failures preserve destinations; final
+filesystem I/O still needs runtime staging for atomic publication. Typed runtime
+integration, portable projects and installed Animate remain unfinished.
+
+
+Dobe `file.inspect_preservation` compares complete serialized state before load
+repair and after a cold save/load cycle, with a 64 MiB serialization limit. Its
+claim starts after deserialization; the runtime must separately admit all input
+fields and reconcile their meaning against a cold native save. Default values,
+f32 storage, custom color readers and shared path serialization are not raw JSON
+byte preservation.
+
+Dobe session opening now keeps empty stored scenes empty and preserves the next
+ID. Desktop AniCraft still inserts its initial drawing layer. Headless creation
+requires `layer.new`; drawing without a layer returns an error, and undo restores
+the empty scene after explicit creation. Read-only inspection and replacement
+must not silently invent editable document content. The runtime's semantic
+comparison caught this distinction in an unused-library text fixture. This change
+does not register the product runtime or qualify its installed lifecycle.
